      "message": "You are a highly experienced code reviewer specializing in Git patches. Your\ntask is to analyze the provided Git patch (`patch`) and provide comprehensive\nfeedback.  Focus on identifying potential bugs, inconsistencies, security\nvulnerabilities, and areas for improvement in code style and readability.\nYour response should be detailed and constructive, offering specific suggestions\nfor remediation where applicable. Prioritize clarity and conciseness in your\nfeedback.\n\n# Step by Step Instructions\n\n1.  Read the provided `patch` carefully.  Understand the changes it introduces to the codebase.\n\n2.  Analyze the `patch` for potential issues:\n    * **Functionality:** Does the code work as intended? Are there any bugs or unexpected behavior?\n    * **Security:** Are there any security vulnerabilities introduced by the patch?\n    * **Style:** Does the code adhere to the project\u0027s coding style guidelines? Is it readable and maintainable?\n    * **Consistency:** Are there any inconsistencies with existing code or design patterns?\n    * **Testing:** Does the patch include sufficient tests to cover the changes?\n\n3.  Formulate concise and constructive feedback for each identified issue.  Provide specific suggestions for remediation where possible.\n\n4.  Summarize your findings in a clear and organized manner.  Prioritize critical issues over minor ones.\n\n5.  Review the feedback written so far. Is the feedback comprehensive and sufficiently detailed?\nIf not, go back to step 2, focusing on any areas that require further analysis or clarification.\n If yes, proceed to step 6.\n\n6.  Output the complete review.\n\n\nPatch: \n\"\"\"\nFrom e33961224bda4a542c76c925d0d242d636b20021 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001\nDate: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:09:10 +0000\nSubject: [PATCH] Add a JavaScript test lane\n\nGitiles ships JavaScript to the browser and has no way to test any of\nit. This adds a way, with as little machinery as the problem allows.\n\nThe rule\n--------\n\nrules_nodejs is already a declared dependency, and its module registers\nhermetic Node toolchains for every supported platform, so\ntools/js_test.bzl needs no new dependency and no change to MODULE.bazel.\nThe rule takes Node from the runtime toolchain type, which Bazel selects\nfor the platform the test runs on, not from the toolchain type it\nselects for the platform build actions run on. A rule is needed at all\nbecause rules_nodejs 6.x ships toolchains only -- nodejs_test moved to\naspect_rules_js -- and because Bazel 9 removed the native sh_test that\ncould otherwise have wrapped a runner. What is left is small: a launcher\nthat invokes `node --test`, passes the `args` attribute and --test_arg\nthrough, and writes a JUnit report when Bazel asks for one.\n\nTests are written against node:test and node:assert, which are part of\nthe runtime. There is no package manager, no lockfile, no node_modules\nand no configuration file to drift.\n\nThey live in jstests/ at the root. javatests/ mirrors java/ package\nfor package, and the code under test is not Java and does not live\nunder java/ -- it is served out of resources/. Putting JavaScript\ntests inside that mirror would make the mirror mean two things. A\nsibling directory named for the language does not, and it reads the\nsame as javatests/ to anyone already used to the tree.\n\nThe smoke test\n--------------\n\nBuild glue that nothing exercises is build glue nobody knows is broken,\nso the rule arrives with a test of its own two promises: that the Node\nrunning the tests is the toolchain\u0027s and not one found on PATH, and that\na file listed in `data` is readable at its workspace-relative path even\nwhen it comes from another package. Both are environment-dependent, and\nneither is visible in the source of a test that merely passes.\n\nCoverage of the JavaScript itself follows in the changes that introduce\nthe code worth covering.\n\nVerification\n------------\n\n  bazel test //...\n\npasses and picks the new target up with no further configuration.\n\nThe lane also runs in CI rather than only locally. The gitiles-build job\nruns `bazelisk test //...` and fails on a non-zero, non-four exit, so a\nfailing JavaScript test fails the job. That was confirmed by observing\nVerified-1 on Change 646024, a throwaway change stacked on this one\nwhose only difference is one deliberately inverted assertion.\n\nBug: 564987520\nTAG\u003dagy\nCONV\u003d0b3e4b30-d902-4587-8bb2-ce23109d6955\nCONV\u003d60d1bfdf-34a4-460e-85ab-0f251e052543\nChange-Id: I11c6bbcf18c548f7507861be2fe71727796ca155\n---\n\ndiff --git a/jstests/BUILD b/jstests/BUILD\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..a18ee55\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/jstests/BUILD\n@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+load(\"//tools:js_test.bzl\", \"js_test\")\n+\n+js_test(\n+    name \u003d \"js_test_rule_test\",\n+    srcs \u003d [\"js_test_rule_test.js\"],\n+    data \u003d [\"//resources/com/google/gitiles\"],\n+)\ndiff --git a/jstests/js_test_rule_test.js b/jstests/js_test_rule_test.js\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..f217216\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/jstests/js_test_rule_test.js\n@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@\n+// Tests the js_test rule\u0027s own contract.\n+//\n+// The rule is build glue, and build glue that nothing exercises is build glue\n+// nobody knows is broken. Two promises are worth pinning, because both are\n+// environment-dependent and neither is visible in the source of a test that\n+// merely passes:\n+//\n+//   1. A hermetic Node resolves from the toolchain rules_nodejs registers, on\n+//      whatever machine the test runs on. Nothing is downloaded by hand and\n+//      no system node is consulted.\n+//   2. A file listed in `data` is readable at its workspace-relative path,\n+//      even when it comes from another package.\n+//\n+// Without (1) the tests run on whatever node a machine happens to have, if\n+// any; without (2) every test that reads the code under test fails in the\n+// same confusing way.\n+\n+const {test} \u003d require(\u0027node:test\u0027);\n+const assert \u003d require(\u0027node:assert\u0027);\n+const fs \u003d require(\u0027node:fs\u0027);\n+const path \u003d require(\u0027node:path\u0027);\n+\n+test(\u0027runs on the toolchain Node, not a node found on PATH\u0027, () \u003d\u003e {\n+  // execPath is the resolved binary. The runfiles entry for the toolchain\n+  // Node is a symlink to wherever Bazel fetched it, which is outside the\n+  // runfiles tree and varies with cache configuration, so its location\n+  // cannot be pinned portably. What can be pinned is that it is not\n+  // whatever a bare `node` would resolve to. Where no system node exists, a\n+  // launcher that fell back to one would not start at all.\n+  const running \u003d fs.realpathSync(process.execPath);\n+  for (const dir of (process.env.PATH || \u0027\u0027).split(path.delimiter)) {\n+    const candidate \u003d path.resolve(dir, \u0027node\u0027);\n+    if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) {\n+      assert.notStrictEqual(fs.realpathSync(candidate), running,\n+          `running the system node at ${candidate}`);\n+    }\n+  }\n+});\n+\n+test(\u0027can read a data dependency from another package\u0027, () \u003d\u003e {\n+  const css \u003d fs.readFileSync(\u0027resources/com/google/gitiles/static/base.css\u0027, \u0027utf8\u0027);\n+  assert.ok(css.length \u003e 0);\n+});\ndiff --git a/tools/js_test.bzl b/tools/js_test.bzl\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..855d859\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/tools/js_test.bzl\n@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@\n+\"\"\"A test rule for plain JavaScript, using Node\u0027s built-in test runner.\n+\n+Gitiles already depends on `rules_nodejs`, whose module registers a hermetic\n+Node toolchain for every supported platform, so this rule needs no new\n+dependency and no `MODULE.bazel` change. `rules_nodejs` 6.x ships toolchains\n+only -- `nodejs_test` lives in `aspect_rules_js` -- hence the rule here.\n+\n+Tests are written against `node:test` and `node:assert`, which are part of the\n+runtime. There is no package manager, no lockfile and no `node_modules`.\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+# Bazel passes XML_OUTPUT_FILE when it wants a JUnit report; without it a test\n+# failure is just a red target with a log to go read. \"$@\" carries the `args`\n+# attribute and --test_arg, e.g. --test_arg\u003d--test-name-pattern\u003d...\n+_LAUNCHER \u003d \"\"\"#!/bin/sh\n+set -eu\n+if [ -n \"${{XML_OUTPUT_FILE:-}}\" ]; then\n+  exec ./{node} --test --test-reporter\u003djunit \\\\\n+    --test-reporter-destination\u003d\"$XML_OUTPUT_FILE\" \\\\\n+    --test-reporter\u003dspec --test-reporter-destination\u003dstdout \"$@\" {tests}\n+fi\n+exec ./{node} --test \"$@\" {tests}\n+\"\"\"\n+\n+def _js_test_impl(ctx):\n+    # The runtime toolchain is selected for the target platform, where the\n+    # test runs; toolchain_type is selected for the exec platform of actions.\n+    node \u003d ctx.toolchains[\"@rules_nodejs//nodejs:runtime_toolchain_type\"].nodeinfo.node\n+    launcher \u003d ctx.actions.declare_file(ctx.label.name + \".sh\")\n+    ctx.actions.write(\n+        output \u003d launcher,\n+        is_executable \u003d True,\n+        content \u003d _LAUNCHER.format(\n+            node \u003d node.short_path,\n+            tests \u003d \" \".join([f.short_path for f in ctx.files.srcs]),\n+        ),\n+    )\n+    runfiles \u003d ctx.runfiles(files \u003d ctx.files.srcs + ctx.files.data + [node])\n+    runfiles \u003d runfiles.merge_all([\n+        dep[DefaultInfo].default_runfiles\n+        for dep in ctx.attr.srcs + ctx.attr.data\n+    ])\n+    return [DefaultInfo(executable \u003d launcher, runfiles \u003d runfiles)]\n+\n+js_test \u003d rule(\n+    doc \u003d \"Runs `node --test` over `srcs` with `data` available in runfiles.\",\n+    implementation \u003d _js_test_impl,\n+    test \u003d True,\n+    attrs \u003d {\n+        \"srcs\": attr.label_list(\n+            doc \u003d \"Test files, passed to `node --test`.\",\n+            allow_files \u003d [\".js\"],\n+            allow_empty \u003d False,\n+            mandatory \u003d True,\n+        ),\n+        \"data\": attr.label_list(\n+            doc \u003d \"Files the tests read at run time, such as the code under test.\",\n+            allow_files \u003d True,\n+        ),\n+    },\n+    toolchains \u003d [\"@rules_nodejs//nodejs:runtime_toolchain_type\"],\n+)\n\n\"\"\"\nIMPORTANT NOTE: Start directly with the output, do not output any delimiters.\nTake a Deep Breath, read the instructions again, read the inputs again. Each instruction is crucial and must be executed with utmost care and attention to detail.\n\nReview:",
